---
title: Ask the assistant about your application mail
date: 2026-07-31
summary: The assistant can now read the labels already on your inbox, so "what's happening with my interview invitations?" is a question you can just ask instead of scrolling.
type: changelog
tags:
  - assistant
  - inbox
draft: false
---

Your [inbox](/my/inbox) has been sorting itself for a while: every message that arrives gets
a label — interview invitation, rejection, offer, assessment — and the ones we can match get
attached to the application they belong to. Until now you could only see that by opening the
inbox and scrolling.

The [assistant](/my/assistant) can read it now. Ask **"what's happening with my
applications?"** and it answers from the labels rather than by reading your mail to you: how
many invitations are waiting, how much has been rejected, what nothing has judged yet. Then
you can narrow it — "show me the interview invitations" — and it lists them with who wrote,
when, and which application each belongs to.

It can also do the tidying that piles up:

- **Work through the suggestions.** Mail is only attached automatically when the match is
  unambiguous; everything else waits for you to confirm. The assistant can walk that queue.
- **Fix a link**, or attach a message to the right application.
- **Record an application from a message.** When an employer writes about something you
  never tracked, it can create that application and attach the mail in one step — dated by
  the message, not by today, so the elapsed-silence count stays honest.

Two things it deliberately cannot do. It **never marks your mail read** — the unread count
is yours, and an assistant sweeping the backlog would quietly zero it. And it **cannot send
mail**, from here or anywhere else. That second one is not an oversight: a recruiter's email
is text written by someone else, and the safest answer to a message that tries to instruct
the assistant is that there is nowhere for it to send anything.

Available to everyone signed in, wherever your mail comes from — Gmail, your freehire
mailbox, or pushed in by your own mail client.
